HST Office: a ribbon toolbox shows all of its item windows.

On Windows at 125 % text the native boxes ask for more height than the ribbon's 24 px row (fill style,
line width and the picture's colour boxes 24-27 px, the font boxes 28 px). The toolbox centres each item
on its line, which follows its tallest item, so a box sat 1-3 px below the toolbox's top or was taller
than the toolbox, and the toolbox cut its bottom (a box shown 20-23 px of 24-28). A ribbon toolbox now
(owner drawn, or laid out by the v3 layout, which makes every one of its toolboxes stretch) places each
item window inside its own height: as high as the window up to the toolbox's inner height, moved up just
enough to end inside. A ribbon toolbox the layout gives another height (it formats at its own height, then
gets the row's) formats again, so the fit sees the height it shows (the picture's Graf boxes stayed 26-27 px
in a 24 px toolbox). Boxes keep their native drawing; nothing changes where they fit (100 %).

- vcl/inc/HstRibbonItemFit.hxx (new, plain arithmetic, unit tested), vcl/source/window/toolbox.cxx (ImplFormat,
  Resize).

--- /dev/null
+++ b/vcl/inc/HstRibbonItemFit.hxx
@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+/* -*- Mode: C++; tab-width: 4; indent-tabs-mode: nil; c-basic-offset: 4 -*- */
+/*
+ * This Source Code Form is subject to the terms of the Mozilla Public
+ * License, v. 2.0. If a copy of the MPL was not distributed with this
+ * file, You can obtain one at http://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/.
+ */
+
+#pragma once
+
+#include <algorithm>
+
+namespace hst::ribbon
+{
+/** The vertical place of a window in a ribbon toolbox item: its top and height in device px. */
+struct ItemSpan
+{
+    long top = 0;
+    long height = 0;
+};
+
+/** Where an item window of a ribbon toolbox goes so that the toolbox shows all of it: the toolbox shows the band
+    [nInnerTop, nInnerTop + nInner). A native box asks for more height when the text is larger (Windows at 125 %:
+    26-28 px in a 24 px row), and the toolbox's line, centred on the tallest item, put it lower than the band; the
+    toolbox then cut its bottom. The window keeps its height up to the band's, and moves up just enough to end in
+    it. A band not laid out yet (nInner <= 0) leaves it as it is. */
+inline ItemSpan fitItemWindow(long nTop, long nHeight, long nInnerTop, long nInner)
+{
+    if (nInner <= 0)
+        return { nTop, nHeight };
+    const long nFit = std::min(nHeight, nInner);
+    return { std::clamp(nTop, nInnerTop, nInnerTop + nInner - nFit), nFit };
+}
+}
+
+/* vim:set shiftwidth=4 softtabstop=4 expandtab: */
